  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 347 AM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nAs a mid-level trough pushes offshore of the northeastern United \nStates, the stout mid-level ridge in place across the western \nAtlantic waters will flatten and lose influence over our region by \nMonday morning. The enhancement of a surface low offshore of the \nnortheastern United States will provide enough forcing for the \naforementioned frontal boundary across Central Florida and the Gulf \nto slowly slide southward across South Florida during the day. With \nall of the best dynamics now well removed from the region, \nconvective activity on Monday is forecast to be much more sparse in \nnature. That being said, maximized ascent and residual moisture \nalong the boundary could still set the stage for some isolated to \nscattered shower and thunderstorm activity. High temperatures on \nMonday will rise into the upper 80s along the east coast to the \nlower 90s across interior portions of Southwest Florida. \n\nHeading towards Tuesday and Wednesday, a much drier air mass will \npush into the region behind the front as high pressure builds back \ninto the area. This will keep chances of showers and storms \nextremely limited during this time frame, and with the drier air and \nlower dewpoints anticipated, there will be a noticeable drop in \nhumidity during the middle of the week. High temperatures on Tuesday \nand Wednesday will still remain above climatological normals for \nthis time of year.",
